WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:34.740
AVATAR 2:
By now, I’m sure you’ve heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:41.940
about Avatar 2. Against many people’s pessimistic&nbsp;
expectations, Avatar 2 has crushed it at the box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:48.600
office having crossed $1 billion within just&nbsp;
12 days. And by the time you’re watching this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:56.280
it may very well be at $2 or even $3 billion. $3&nbsp;
billion would make it the new box office king,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:02.580
but then again, do you really expect anything&nbsp;
different from James Cameron? What may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:08.820
surprising, however, is that Avatar likely&nbsp;
won’t make all that much profit even if it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:14.460
reaches these record breaking numbers. In fact,&nbsp;
according to Cameron himself, Avatar 2 will have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:20.460
to be the 3rd or 4th highest grossing film&nbsp;
in history just to breakeven. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:28.800
Avatar must cross $2 to $2.2 billion simply to&nbsp;
not lose money. Now, we all know that Avatar 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.820
cost a bunch of money to make, but there’s no&nbsp;
way it cost $2 billion to make right? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.100
that’s correct. While Cameron hasn’t revealed&nbsp;
exact production numbers, current estimates put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:49.200
the film at $250 million. This means that for some&nbsp;
reason, Avatar needs to pull in 8 times its budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.140
at the box office to breakeven. Even if we use&nbsp;
higher estimates for the budget like $460 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.140 --> 00:02:01.260
Avatar would need to pull in over 5 times its&nbsp;
budget at the box office. This makes no sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:07.020
when you consider that films with similar budgets&nbsp;
have far lower breakevens. Take James Bond Spectre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:13.320
for example. Spectre’s production budget was $300&nbsp;
million, but it only needed $650 million at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:18.780
box office to breakeven. Even Avengers Endgame&nbsp;
which cost $400 million to make, only needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:25.740
earn $1.1 to $1.2 billion. In other words, despite&nbsp;
having a similar budget to Endgame if not lower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:31.860
Avatar 2 needs to earn twice as much at the&nbsp;
box office to show a profit. Now, to be fair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.620
Cameron did admit that Avatar 2 doesn’t make&nbsp;
sense from a studio financing perspective and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:43.260
that its the quote “worst business case&nbsp;
in movie history.” That still leaves the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:49.200
question though, where’s all the other money&nbsp;
going? So, here’s the baffling economics of Avatar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:55.920
2 and why Avatar needs to earn $1.5 to $1.75&nbsp;
billion more than its budget just to breakeven?

00:02:55.920 --> 00:03:00.300
UNFAVORABLE THEATER SPLITS:&nbsp;

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.820
Starting off, we have the most obvious factor&nbsp;
eating into Avatar’s profit margins which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.860
splitting revenues with theaters. Now, having&nbsp;
to split revenues isn’t unique to Avatar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:15.780
but there are two characteristics that are unique&nbsp;
to Avatar that make splitting revenues especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:21.540
costly. Here’s the thing, most theater deals are&nbsp;
setup to make the average movie as profitable as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:26.520
possible for both parties. This means that studios&nbsp;
get a larger percentage during the opening weekend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.800
and the first few weeks while theaters get a&nbsp;
larger percentage after week 3 or week 4. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:37.020
makes sense because most movies have a massive&nbsp;
cliff after the first week or two. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:42.000
theaters are able to sustain lower splits during&nbsp;
the initial phases of a movie because showings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.740
are packed and people are buying a bunch of&nbsp;
concessions. But, during the later phases of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:51.900
a movie, theaters need a higher split given that&nbsp;
attendance is much lower and a fewer percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:57.420
tend to buy concessions. This works out great&nbsp;
for studios as well given that they get a larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:02.340
split while a given movie is pulling in the&nbsp;
bulk of its revenue. However, as time passes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.240
they’re willing to settle for a lower split&nbsp;
to maximize the time that a movie stays in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.900
theaters. In terms of numbers, studios generally&nbsp;
start with 55 to 60% of the revenue and they drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:19.380
down to 40 to 45% of the revenue. Sometimes this&nbsp;
ends up being as low as even 30%. While this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:25.440
an ingenious design, it’s not all that beneficial&nbsp;
for Avatar given that it doesn’t perform like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.200
average big budget film. You see, Avatar isn’t the&nbsp;
type of film to have massive opening weekends like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:37.800
Marvel. It’s much more of a slow burn. This is&nbsp;
why we saw so many articles early on suggesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.200
that Avatar was getting destroyed at the box&nbsp;
office when in reality that wasn’t the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:49.440
And this is abundantly clear when we compare the&nbsp;
staying power of Endgame and the original Avatar.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:56.220
Endgame started off with multiple 9 figure days&nbsp;
while Avatar barely had $20 million days. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:01.320
we look further down the line, however, this&nbsp;
dynamic is flipped on its head. By day 51,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:07.320
Avatar was still having 8 figure days, while&nbsp;
Endgame was barely achieving 7 figures. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.660
this is great news for Avatar’s longevity, it’s&nbsp;
not all the great when it comes to theater splits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:17.520
While Endgame likely only enjoyed splits&nbsp;
of 55 or 60% for the first week or two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.500
given that that’s where all the revenue was,&nbsp;
their average take in the US was likely closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:28.440
to 55%. Meanwhile, Avatar is much more likely to&nbsp;
be weighed down by lower splits in later phases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:35.220
and their average take is likely closer to 50%&nbsp;
or maybe even slightly lower. This may not sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:41.220
like a big deal, but when we’re talking about&nbsp;
box office numbers of $2 to $3 billion, we’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:47.640
talking about $100 to $150 million less in studio&nbsp;
earnings despite the same box office numbers. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:53.580
perspective, that margin itself would be enough&nbsp;
to direct the entirety of Batman Begins. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:58.680
due to the mechanics of theater splits, Avatar 2&nbsp;
has to earn a lot more to keep the same amount.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:05.640
UNFAVORABLE GLOBAL SPLITS:
While US theater splits are already a headache&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:11.640
for Avatar 2, that’s just the first of Disney’s&nbsp;
problems with the movie. Another major hurdle that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:17.220
Disney has to deal with is international revenue&nbsp;
splits. Here’s the thing, western products usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:21.900
attract the richest and most well off people&nbsp;
in a given country. Countries are well aware&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:27.060
of this and they want a share of the revenue. This&nbsp;
is why products like the iPhone are hit with a 20%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:33.720
tariff and imported cars are hit with a nasty 100%&nbsp;
tariff in India. Cameron doesn’t get any freebies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:39.420
in this realm and he’s gonna have to pony up an&nbsp;
entertainment tax of anywhere between 2 and 50%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:45.600
probably closer to 50% in India. I’ve been&nbsp;
picking on India, but they’re by no means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:52.320
that only ones with such policies. The UK, for&nbsp;
example, has a tax of 20%. Now, technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.840
it’s the end customer who has to pay for these&nbsp;
taxes, but it would stupid for studios to not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.660
subsidize these taxes whatsoever. Otherwise,&nbsp;
they’ll end up like Apple with 4% market share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:09.600
in the second most populated country in the world.&nbsp;
Disney obviously doesn’t want to do this because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:14.580
unlike something like Top Gun, the vast majority&nbsp;
of Avatar’s fanbase is actually international.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:20.100
With the original Avatar for example, only&nbsp;
a quarter of its box office actually came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:25.740
from America while over $2 billion came from other&nbsp;
countries. To maintain this international success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:31.380
however, Avatar 2 tickets have to be somewhat&nbsp;
reasonably priced even after massive taxes of 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:38.220
or 50%. The only way to accomplish this though is&nbsp;
to sacrifice margins significantly. Usually, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:45.120
reduces profit margins to somewhere between 20 and&nbsp;
40%. The UK for example is on average 30% while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:52.800
China and Russia are 25% and 38% respectively.&nbsp;
Now, Avatar is not the average movie, so it’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:58.080
likely be able to boast a larger international&nbsp;
premium than the average movie. But, even if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:05.760
call it 40%, that’s a big drop from the domestic&nbsp;
50%. Again, a 10% difference is worth $200 to $300&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:11.340
million at Avatar’s scale, enough money to direct&nbsp;
a wholenother Avatar. If we apply these numbers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:16.020
Cameron’s breakeven of $2 billion, we can get a&nbsp;
rough estimate of just how much the studio will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:21.720
actually earn. As I’m making this video, the&nbsp;
global domestic split for Avatar 2 is roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:28.560
70/30, so we’ll just roll with that. This means&nbsp;
that when Avatar 2 reaches $2 billion, the global&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:34.860
draw would be roughly $1.4 billion while the&nbsp;
domestic draw would be $600 million. Using our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:41.460
box office splits of 40% and 50%, we get a global&nbsp;
keep of $560 million and a domestic keep of $300&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:48.480
million. This adds up to a total retained revenue&nbsp;
of $860 million. And remember, that’s with our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:54.600
Avatar premium tickets estimation. The real number&nbsp;
may actually be closer to $700 to $800 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.600 --> 00:09:02.040
So, that explains $1.1 to $1.2 billion worth of&nbsp;
the box office, but that still leaves us with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:08.340
a $600 million gap between $860 million and the&nbsp;
budget of $250 million. So, what happened there?

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:12.720
OTHER EXPENSES:&nbsp;

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:18.120
Well, the first thing that we should point out is&nbsp;
that it’s highly unlikely that Cameron actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:23.820
stuck to the initial budget of $250 million. The&nbsp;
initial Avatar itself cost $237 million to make,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:28.920
and if you’ve seen the new Avatar, you know&nbsp;
that there’s no way that this film only cost an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:34.860
extra $13 million to make. If that’s actually the&nbsp;
case, Cameron actually pulled off a miracle. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:40.080
more realistically, the film actually cost $460&nbsp;
million to make like the higher estimates suggest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:47.280
With an estimated retention of $860 million, this&nbsp;
only leaves us with $400 million and there’s still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:51.660
plenty of expenses left like marketing. The&nbsp;
original Avatar is estimated to have spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:58.680
$150 million on marketing. And that was when&nbsp;
Avatar was a completely unproven concept. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:03.480
that Avatar has since become the world’s highest&nbsp;
grossing film and that Disney has acquired the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:09.000
franchise and that they need an insane box office&nbsp;
number to breakeven, I suspect that Disney will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:13.980
be more than willing to outspend the last avatar&nbsp;
in terms of marketing. The marketing budget is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:19.800
likely $200 to $250 million. We’ll call it $200&nbsp;
million to be conservative which leaves us with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:24.900
another $200 million. About $100 million of this&nbsp;
will go to random miscellaneous expenses like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:30.420
interest rates. Studios generally fund a lot of&nbsp;
their production using debt, and this was likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:36.000
especially expensive on Avatar 2 given that it’s&nbsp;
been in production forever meaning that there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.700
plenty of time for interest to stack on. Not to&nbsp;
mention the recent rate hikes. Another place in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:46.800
which Disney will lose some money is currency&nbsp;
fluctuations and exchange fees. You can’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:52.080
convert billions of dollars worth of foreign&nbsp;
currencies to US dollars for free. Disney will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.940
also have to spend some money on miscellaneous&nbsp;
distribution expenses and sales agent fees. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:01.320
takes time and effort to actually get your films&nbsp;
into the hands of thousands of theaters around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:06.840
world in dozens of formats and languages. Putting&nbsp;
aside these miscellaneous expenses, we still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:12.180
a $100 million and this will likely be paid out&nbsp;
to large stakeholders in the project. This usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:18.300
includes major actors. For example, Cameron&nbsp;
offered 10% of Avatar profits to Matt Damon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:24.120
if he joined the team. Avatar doesn’t star anyone&nbsp;
like Leonardo Dicaprio or Brad Pitt or of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:30.240
Matt Damon. But, Sam Worthington does get 5% of&nbsp;
the profits and other major characters like Zoe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:36.660
Saldana may have similar agreements. It should be&nbsp;
noted that these figures are based on profits, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:41.940
gross box office numbers, but there may very well&nbsp;
be stakeholders that are paid off the topline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:48.090
One of the most notable stakeholders is of course&nbsp;
Jimbo himself. He said that Avatar would have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.090 --> 00:11:53.220
pull in $2 billion for the studio to make money,&nbsp;
not for himself to make money. And given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:58.920
Cameron is worth $700 million, he’s clearly no&nbsp;
stranger to making lucrative deals with studios.

00:11:58.920 --> 00:12:04.980
THE ECONOMICS OF AVATAR 2:
In the end, while it may be baffling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.980 --> 00:12:10.860
to hear that a $250 million movie needs $2 billion&nbsp;
to breakeven, it actually makes a lot more sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:15.300
when we take a closer look and account for things&nbsp;
like the movie going over budget, theater splits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:20.760
global splits, marketing, distribution fees,&nbsp;
economic stuff, and key stakeholders. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:25.800
at the end of the day, Cameron didn’t make&nbsp;
this film to make money. If that was truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:30.600
his main goal, he wouldn’t have waited 13&nbsp;
years to release another movie. For him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:36.840
this is much more of a passion project and about&nbsp;
bringing his vision to reality. With that being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:42.120
said though, we can’t just completely ignore the&nbsp;
economics because that’s what keeps the lights on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:46.980
and allows Cameron to do all this crazy stuff.&nbsp;
Fortunately for Avatar though, it doesn’t look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:52.080
like it’ll have much trouble breaking even.&nbsp;
And who knows, it might even become the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:56.760
world’s highest grossing movie especially if it&nbsp;
has anywhere near as much sticking power as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:01.440
original. And this should go without saying,&nbsp;
but if you haven’t yet seen this movie yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.440 --> 00:13:07.320
you absolutely should, but that’s just what I&nbsp;
think. Are you a fan of Avatar? Comment that&nbsp;&nbsp;

